          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            We follow
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SS23QM"} SS23-QM
          %h3#the_problem The problem
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit+diagram"} quantum circuit diagram
            (originally: “quantum computational network”,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "quantum+circuit#Deutsch89"} Deutsch 1989
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            (
            %a{:href => "quantum+circuit#AbramskyCoecke04"}> Abramsky &amp; Coecke 2004
            ) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic+gate"} quantum gates
            mapping between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+quantum+state"} pure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
          %p
            A generic pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
            might look as follows:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 10px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumCircuitScheme-221025c.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            Here, for instance, the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gate"}> quantum gate
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 34
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{34}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} linear operator
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \oplus \mathscr{H}
          %p
            Notice that the quantum circuit is understood to be the actual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+transformation"} linear transformation
            which it encodes: The compositeness of the diagram encodes how (e.g. in which order) available
            = succeed "-operations" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gate"} quantum gate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computer"} quantum computer
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
            and one also speaks of the “quantum circuit model of quantum computation” (e.g.
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#NielsenChuang00"} Nielsen &amp; Chuang 2000, §II.4.6
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Miszczak11"} Miszczak 2011, §3
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Miszczak12"} 2012, §4.3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BenetiCasati18"} Beneti &amp; Casati 2018, §3.2
          %p
            Often the basic Hilbert space building block here is taken to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex
            2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"}> dimensional
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \,\coloneqq\, \mathbb{C}^2
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/q-bits"} q-bits
          %p
            Typically all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            involved, and hence also the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible"} invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reversible+computation"} reversible computation
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary
            quantum state evolution in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong But more generally
            and certainly in the broader context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            (such as in formulating the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+teleportation+protocol"} quantum teleportation protocol
            and similar), quantum circuits are admitted to cross the
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                quantum-classical divide
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/quantum-classical+divide"} ?
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+preparation"} quantum state preparation
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classically+controlled+quantum+gates"} classically controlled quantum gates
                \.
          %p
            All three of these appear already in basic examples such as the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+teleportation+protocol"} quantum teleportation protocol
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QBitQuantumTeleportationProtocol-221109.jpg", :width => "830px"}/
          %p
            Yet more generally — notably for any plausible implementation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
            — one needs to allow for
            = succeed "-results" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"} dynamic lifting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithms"} algorithms
            running on a universal classical computer which schedule further
            = succeed "-execution," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SQRAMschema-WithDynLifting.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                SQRAM model adapted from
                %a{:href => "#NagarajanPapanikolaouWilliams07"} Nagarajan, Papanikolaou  Williams 2007, Fig 1
          %p
            The conceptual subtlety with fully formalizing these common ideas is that
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reversible+computation"} reversible
            (due to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+collapse"} quantum state collapse
            involved) but also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+computation"} non-deterministic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
          %p
            Concretely, the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+postulate"}> projection postulate
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            asserts (
