          %p
            There are different ways to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type
            the quantum measurement, taking into account the non-deterministic nature of its outcome:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Regarding the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi B
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_{b \colon B} \mathscr{H}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} logical disjunction
                (“or”) of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
                into the direct sum whose
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_b
              %blockquote
                %p
                  This choice of typing appears (briefly) in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "quantum+measurement#Selinger04"} Selinger 2004
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.mathstat.dal.ca/~selinger/papers/qpl.pdf#page=39"} p. 39
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"} Quipper
            %li
              %p
                Regarding the measurement outcome
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B
                as the observed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                of the actual quantum collapse, one may regard the collapse projection as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependently typed
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Getting from previous option back to this one is known in the the
                  = succeed "-community" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"} Quipper
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "Quipper#ReferencesDynamicLifting"} dynamic lifting
                  (namely “of the measured bits back into the context”)
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PossibleTypingsOfQuantumMeasurement-221025.jpg", :width => "560px"}/
          %p
            Both of these options naturally emerge and are naturally unified in the “
            %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#ModalQuantumLogic"}> Quantum Modal Logic
            ” inherent to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
          %h3#a_solution A solution
          %p
            The following discussion indicates how a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
            for
            = succeed "-processing" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information"} quantum information
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
            consisting of general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            with classical control, including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} de-coherent
            = succeed "-gates" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linearly typed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            which expresses the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/yoga+of+six+operations"}> yoga of six operations
            ”, such as is the case in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+homotopy+type+theory"} linear homotopy type theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Riley22Thesis"} Riley 2022
          %p
            This works by regarding classically controlled
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            as having
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type"} linear data types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            on classical control parameters and on measurement results, and then making the following key observations on
            %strong
              %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#ModalQuantumLogic"} modal quantum logic
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+types"} dependent linear types
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"}> necessity
                ”
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi C
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi C
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi C
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C \coloneqq (p_C)^\ast (p_C)_\ast
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
                dependent on a given classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                (which includes classical control parameters as well as eventual
                = succeed "-outcomes" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                of the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo □
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Kleisli+category"} co-Kleisli category
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo □
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit
                (taking a linear type “out of the comonad”) reflects the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+collapse"} quantum state collapse
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo □
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comultiplication"} comultiplication
                encodes the
                = succeed "-principle." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/external+tensor+product"} external tensor product
                of dependent linear types gives the compilation of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
                into
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
                = succeed "-calculus" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
                %a{:href => "finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories#AbramskyCoecke04"} Abramsky &amp; Coecke 2004
                with respect to the plain
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %p
            All constructions and proofs here flow readily from just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operation+yoga"} six operation yoga
            and (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Kleisli+category"}> co-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"}> Kleisli theory
            \. One key point is
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NecessityRespectsExternalTensorProduct"} (?)
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            respects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/external+tensor+product"} external tensor product
            – so that these classically controlled quantum circuits are unambiguously defined –, under the assumption that it coincides with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} dual
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
            modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◊
                    %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge_C \mathscr{H}_C
            on the quantum data types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}_\bullet
            involved – hence (in full generality) when the linear types are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidexterity
            and specifically (in the traditional models) when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} quantum state spaces
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
          %p
            Aspects of the following have a resemblance to some variants and categorical interpretations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"}> Quipper
            ” (see the references
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Quipper#ReferencesDependentLinearTypesAndCategoricalSemantics"} there
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#NoticeTheTypingOfAQbit"} below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Quipper#ReferencesDynamicLifting"} there
            = succeed "-modality" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#idea_modal_quantum_logic_via_linear_homotopy_types Idea: Modal quantum logic via linear homotopy types
          %p
            We assume a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorially
            obtains (as anticipated in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Schreiber14"} Schreiber 2014
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.7041.pdf#page=39"} §3.2
            and established in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Riley22Thesis"} Riley 2022
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "https://digitalcollections.wesleyan.edu/islandora/object/ir:3269"} §2.4
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                from a classical (“intuitionistic”)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X : Type
                a (large) type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi LinType
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinType_X
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                (cf.
                = succeed ")," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sets"} quantum sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \otimes (-)
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"}> tensor product
                ) and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,\,-]
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"}> internal hom
                );
            %li
              %p
                from a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
