            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QCwthLHT-BaseChange-221001.jpg", :width => "730px"}/
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CharacteristicOfLinearTypes-221108jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %h3#ClassicalModalLogic Classical modal logic
          %p
            First observe that (without imposing a further condition) also classical (“non-linear”)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            provides an example of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/yoga+of+six+operations"} yoga of six operations
            (actually five distinct operations, in the present case of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+contexts"} Wirthmüller contexts
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
                given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+coproduct"} dependent coproduct
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
                given by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
          %p
            In this classical situation, the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"}> co
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◊
                    %mi C
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge_C \dashv \Box_C
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            is readily found (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory"} here
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            and the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            of a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            which regards the potential measurement outcomes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : C
            as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds+semantics"}> possible worlds
            ”:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QCwthLHT-ClassicalNecessity-221003.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            In fact there is (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#RelationToPotentiality"} here
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} co-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monadic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            associated with any
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TheFourModalitiesOfBaseChange-221101b.jpg", :width => "370"}/
          %p Here
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the definiteness-modality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
                is also known as the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/writer+comonad"} writer comonad
            %li
              %p
                the randomness-modality
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ◯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
                is also known as the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
          %p
            We recover the meaning of these expressions in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+preparation"} quantum state preparation
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#QuantumMeasurement"} below
          %p
            (In general there may be richer dependency on classical contexts. In the next simplest case the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            is along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            map out of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of classical types, which makes one of them label potential upcoming measurement results as before, while the other encodes further dependency on classical parameters, such as on previous measurement results:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QCwthLHT-RelativeBaseChange-221003.jpg", :width => "670px"}/
          %p
            This plays a role for classically-controlled quantum gates,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ClassicallyControlledQuantumGate"} below
          %h3#QuantumModalLogic Quantum modal logic
          %p
            This general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"} possible worlds
            %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory"} found inside
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            becomes subject to one curious further rule as we pass to genuinely
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+homotopy+type+theory"} linear homotopy type theory
          %p
            Namely, assuming that the potential measurement outcomes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \colon C
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            — which is the case of relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            —, the linearity or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stability
            axiom implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            down from the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            becomes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidextrous
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_\bullet
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            computed by the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            computed by the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %em agree
            (the situation of a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"}> biproduct
            ”):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BC
                  %mo :
                  %mi FinSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo •
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi LinType
                    %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ◊
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo •
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo □
                  %msub
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo •
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  BC : FinSet
                  ,\;\;
                  \\mathscr{H}_\bullet
                  \\,\colon\,
                  LinType_B
                  \\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\vdash
                  \\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\lozenge \mathscr{H}_\bullet
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\Box \mathscr{H}_\bullet
                  \\,.
          %p
            Curiously, in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gell-Mann+principle"} Gell-Mann principle
            of quantum physics:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mtext The
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext possible
                        %mtd
                          %mtext is
                        %mtd
                          %mtext necessary
                        %mtd
                          %mtext and hence
                        %mtd
                          %mtext actualized
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ◊
                          %msub
                            %mi ℋ
                            %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %mo □
                          %msub
                            %mi ℋ
                            %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mphantom
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                  %mo −
                              %mi ϵ
                              %mphantom
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                  %mo −
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ℋ
                            %mrow
                              %mo •
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mtext with some probability
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\llap{\text{The}}\; \text{possible}
                  \&amp;
                  \\text{is}
                  \&amp;
                  \\text{necessary}
                  \&amp;
                  \\text{and hence}
                  \&amp;
                  \\text{actualized}
                  \\\
                  \\lozenge \mathcal{H}_\bullet
                  \&amp;
                  \\simeq
                  \&amp;
                  \\Box \mathcal{H}_\bullet
                  \&amp;
                  \\xrightarrow{\phantom{--} \epsilon \phantom{--}}
                  \&amp;
                  \\mathcal{H}_{
                  \\bullet
                  \\;
                  \\mathrlap{\text{with some probability}}
                  }
                  }
          %p
            Concretely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+homotopy+type+theory"} linear homotopy type theory
            of
            %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Riley22Thesis"} Riley 2022
            should have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-topos
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+spectrum"} parameterized
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\mathbb{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantization+via+Linear+homotopy+types"}> S. 2014, Ex. 3.11
            ) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
            embed into this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            under passage to their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectra"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectra
            (see the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
          #eq:NotationalConvention.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext It is convenient and meaningful to declare the notational conventions to:
                  %mphantom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
