              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/q-bits"} q-bits
          %h3#QuantumGates Quantum gates
          %p
            With the above modal quantum logic, we may naturally type (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/controlled+quantum+gates"}> controlled
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            as follows:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ControlledQuantumGateTyping-230808.jpg", :width => "750px"}/
          %p
            As indicated at the bottom, we may understand classically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/controlled+quantum+gates"} controlled quantum gates
            as
            = succeed "-effectful" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
          %p
            (In the bottom left we assume that the state spaces themselves are independent of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \colon W
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_\bullet \,=\, \mathscr{H}
          %p
            Next we see that the
            = succeed "-effect" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
          %h3#QuantumMeasurement Quantum measurement
          %p
            Specifically,
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C
            %strong actualization is the measurement process
            – in that the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit
            implements exactly the rule for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} wavefunction collapse
            (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "wave+function+collapse#Lüders51"} Lüders 1951
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "wave+function+collapse#vonNeumann32"} von Neumann 1932, §III.3 and §VI
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurements"} quantum measurements
            in the
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{H} \coloneqq \Box_C \mathcal{H}_\bullet
            which is encoded by its direct sum decomposition
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}_\bullet
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QCwthLHT-QuantumNecessity-221021.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p Hence we may type quantum measurement gates as follows:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuantumMeasurementGateTyping.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %p
            As indicated at the bottom, we may understand quantum measurement in this form as the
            %em
              %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#MonadModulesInIdeaSection"} effect handling
            (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#RelationToPotentiality"} potentiality modal logic
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QMeasurementViaIndefinitenessHandling-221109c.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            Notice that this process may be understood as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"}> dynamic lifting
            ” of
            = succeed "-results" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            (here:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B =
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bool"} Bool
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            There is another possible typing of quantum measurement, where a measurement lands in the random
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"} reader monad
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PossibleTypingsOfQuantumMeasurement-221028.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            These two are related by the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            (being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturality+square"} naturality square
            of the necessity-counit on the possibility-unit, see at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "S5+modal+logic#HaxagonOfEpistemicEntailments"} this remark
          #eq:MeasurementViaWritedComonad.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuantumEpistemicHexagon-230806.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Given all this, the
            %a{:href => "Kleisli+category#KleisliEquivalence"} Kleisli equivalence
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_B
            is the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deferred+measurement+principle"} deferred measurement principle
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DeferredMeasurementAsKleisliEquiv-221027b.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#QCwthLHT-QBits Example: Modal typing of basic QBit gates
          %p
            We spell out in terms of the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            found inside
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            the traditional zoo of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            and basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
            acting on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
            for their classically-controlled versions (as such somewhat akin to parts of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"} Quipper
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The typing of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CNOT+gate"} CNOT gate
            in its two incarnations as a classically or a quantumly
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/controlled+quantum+gate"} controlled quantum gate
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CNOTgate-typing-230809.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %p
            Here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            on one
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi QBit
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QBit
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QBitMeasurementTyping-230809.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
          %p
            and their combination, in accord with the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deferred+measurement+principle"} deferred measurement principle
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CNOT-DeferredMeasurement-230809.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %h2#TheQuantumProgrammingLanguage Quantum Programming Language
          %p
            With all elements of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
            formulated via (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"}> co
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            constructed inside a language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+types"} dependent linear types
            as above, we obtain an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+specific+embedded+programming+language"} embedded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
            for quantum circuits with classical control and effects.
          %h3#quantumclassical_types_and_modalities Quantum/classical types and modalities
          %p
            We use the following notation for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+system"} type system
            and the quantum/classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            on these.
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_QuantumClassicalDataTypes-221123.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
            Here the expressions in square brackets may be thought of as the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundles"} bundles
            of linear types (for instance semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
            the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectBund"} VectBund
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundles"} complex vector bundles
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+spectrum"} parametrized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\mathbb{C}
              \-
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
          %p
            The classical modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \natural
            and the quantum modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Q}
            send a bundle type to its base and to its “linearized total space”, respectively:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_QuantumClassicalDivide-221123.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            (The quantum monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Q}
            is a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"} relative monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectBund"} VectBund
            is discussed
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "relative+monad#LinearSpan"} there
          %p
            The classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            and its quantum version relative to the (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/external+tensor+product"}> external
            ”) linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            (for more on this see the discussion
            %a{:href => "VectBund#ClosedMonoidalStructures"} here
            at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectBund"} VectBund
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_ProductAndFunctionTypes-221123.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %p
            Notice that all these types are bundles “
            %em> in themselves
            ”, in that we did not invoke (yet) any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            (semantically we speak about a total
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
                tangent
                = succeed "-topos" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
                slice
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Instead, the actual
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            that we need to reflect (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"}> dynamic lifting
            ” of)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            will all be viewed, indirectly, through the lens of
            = succeed "-modales" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+functor"} comparison functor
            which here is an equivalence).
          %p
            The only explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            that we do need to consider is – besides the trivial dependency on the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝟙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{1}
            %em sugar away
            by the following “circle-colon”-notation (which is meant to rhyme on the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            ”-notation for
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+implication"} linear implication
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_QuantumDataDeclaration-221123.jpg", :width => "480px"}/
          %h3#sugaring_modal_dependent_linear_types_to_a_quantum_language Sugaring modal dependent linear types to a quantum language
          %p
            We now obtain a quantum programming language from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            simply by meticulously
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+sugar"} sugaring
          %p
            %a{:href => "#ForDoNotation"} (1)
            the standard
            = succeed "-notation" do
              %code do
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
          %p
            %a{:href => "#NotationForPureEffects"} (2)
            the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"}> co
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"}> pure/return
            \-functions of the various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
          %h4#ForDoNotation Syntax for effect binding
          %p
            We code maps in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} effect monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            by this schema:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_ForDoNotation-221123b.jpg", :width => "440px"}/
          %p This syntax is to closely reflect the fact that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em for
                an input of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} return
                        %mi D
                        %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \return^{\mathcal{E}}_D(d) \colon \mathcal{E}D
            %li
              %p
                which may appear as a contribution
                %em in
                the effectful input data,
            %li
              %p
                the operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} bind
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prog
                        %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bind^{\mathcal{E}}_{\mathrm{prog}}
                %em does
                produce the output
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prog
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{prog}(d)
          %p which prescription completely defines it.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p Beware that common classical notation for exactly the same costruction is a little different:
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ClassicalMonadicDoNotation-221123.jpg", :width => "280px"}/
            %p
              This classical notation is meant to suggest that pure data
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \colon D
              may be “read out” from effectful data
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \colon \mathcal{E}D
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list+monad"} list monad
              and its common relatives in classical programming, it is misleading in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
              and notably so for the quantum monad
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Q}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = \vert \psi \rangle
              is a quantum state like a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/q-bit"} q-bit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Bit
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \colon Bit
              is a classical
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bit"} bit
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ←
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \leftarrow \vert \psi \rangle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
              – in contradiction to the actual nature of the resulting
              = succeed "-operation" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} bind
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prog
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bind^{\mathrm{Q}}_{\mathrm{prog}}
            %p
              Instead, what really happens in Kleisli formalism is that operations are defined on
              %em generators
              for effectful data types
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}(D)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} return
                        %mi D
                        %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \return^{\mathcal{E}}_D(d)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} QBit
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \psi \rangle \colon \QBit
              is generated (here:
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} linearly spanned
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} basis
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert 0 \rangle
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert 1 \rangle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert - \rangle
              as the unit/return operation which regards a classical bit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              as the corresponding basis quantum state
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert b \rangle
          %p
            Moreover, we write an analogous
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code for...do
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_TensorProductForDo-221123.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p (…)
          %h4#NotationForPureEffects Syntax for pure effects
          %p
            Finally, the monad-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} return operations
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo `
                  %mi return
                  %msup
                    %mo `
                    %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} `return`^{\mathcal{E}}(-)
            ” (and the comonad
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} extract operations
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_SyntaxForPurePotentialityEffects-221123.jpg", :width => "540px"}/
          %p
            In the same vein we sugar the constructor for terms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_B
            as follows:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_MeasurementTermConstruction-221123.jpg", :width => "490px"}/
          %h3#example_pseudocode Example pseudo-code
          %h4#basic_qbit_circuit_ingredients Basic qbit circuit ingredients
          %p
            Basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic+gates"} quantum logic gates
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The quantum
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NOT"} NOT
            = succeed "-gate" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrix"} Pauli X
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_PauliXGate-221123.jpg", :width => "170px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+gate"} Hadamard gate
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QS_HadamardGate-221123.jpg", :width => "270px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The
